WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.500
Bash SSH automation often runs from CI/CD systems, ephemeral runners, self-hosted runners, deployment containers, bastions, jump hosts, or administrative workstations.

2
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.680
Each environment changes the risk picture.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:21.340
The same SSH command can be low risk in one context and too broad in another.

4
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.940
CI/CD keys need careful scoping.

5
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:31.640
A key available to every job, branch, pull request, or workflow can grant access in places that do not need it.

6
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:38.060
Secure design scopes credentials to specific jobs, roles, environments, and targets.

7
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.480
Production deployment should require a higher-trust context than ordinary test automation.

8
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.320
Hosted, self-hosted, and ephemeral runners have different concerns.

9
00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.980
A self-hosted runner may retain workspace state or local configuration.

10
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.040
An ephemeral runner may need managed known-hosts provisioning and secret injection.

11
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.900
A deployment container may need a minimal environment that contains only the keys and tools required for that job.

12
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:10.620
Bastions and jump hosts can improve control because they centralize access paths and monitoring.

13
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.200
They also become critical trust points.

14
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:22.900
Access to a bastion, its logs, its authorized keys, and its forwarding rules should be reviewed because many automation paths may depend on it.

15
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.660
Avoid one credential that works everywhere.

16
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.280
Broad credentials are convenient until they need to be rotated, investigated, or revoked.

17
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:39.380
Narrow credentials create more setup work, but they make it easier to enforce environment boundaries and understand which workflow has access to which target.

18
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.780
Cleanup matters.

19
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:49.520
Workspaces, temporary key material, agent state, known-hosts files, and deployment artifacts should not linger longer than needed.

20
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.780
Secure SSH automation includes the runner or bastion environment, not only the ssh command line.
